---
<Base title="n8n pricing (Oct 2026): Cloud vs self-hosted, real monthly cost" description="n8n Cloud costs about $22–56/mo (€20–50) billed annually; self-hosting is free but needs a server from ~$10/mo. Verified prices from n8n, Hetzner, DigitalOcean and Vultr, checked Oct 2026." schema={schema} crumbs={[['Home', '/'], ['n8n pricing', '/pricing/n8n/']]}>
<div class="wrap page-head prose">
  <span class="eyebrow">Pricing guide · Checked on Oct 22, 2026 · prices read Oct 21, 2026</span>
  <h1>n8n pricing: Cloud vs self-hosted, real monthly cost</h1>
  <p class="muted">I read every price below off the vendor’s own page on {CHECKED} and linked it, so you can check me. I went back over the page on Oct 22, 2026. Prices move, so look at the source before you pay for anything. Where something is my opinion rather than a published number, I say so.</p>
  <p class="note"><b>Disclosure:</b> we may later add affiliate links to some providers on this page. There are none yet; all links below are plain links. <a href="/disclosure/">How we make money</a>.</p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>n8n Cloud:</b> Starter <b>≈ $22/mo</b> (€20), Pro <b>≈ $56/mo</b> (€50), both billed annually (<a href={S.pricing}>n8n pricing</a>). n8n looks after the servers, upgrades and backups for you.</li>
    <li><b>Self-hosted Community edition:</b> $0 license. You pay for a VPS, roughly <b>$10–24/mo</b> for a 2–4 GB server on Vultr or DigitalOcean, plus your own time on updates, backups and security.</li>
    <li><b>Self-hosted Business:</b> <b>≈ $749/mo</b> (€667) billed annually, for SSO, environments, Git version control and queue mode on your own servers.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section style="padding-top:6px"><div class="wrap">
<h2>n8n Cloud and license plans</h2>
<div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col">Price</th><th scope="col">What you get (from n8n’s page)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>{cloud.map(c => <tr><th scope="row">{c.plan}</th><td><b class="usd">{c.usd}</b><br /><small class="eur">{c.price} (official)</small></td><td>{c.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="note">Source: <a href={S.pricing}>n8n.io/pricing</a>, checked {CHECKED}. n8n lists Cloud prices in euros, billed annually; it bills by workflow executions (one run of a whole workflow, however many steps). All plans include unlimited users, workflows and integrations. I didn’t verify the monthly-billing price, so I’ve left it out.</p>
<p class="note">{FX_NOTE}</p>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
<h2>What server does self-hosted n8n need?</h2>
<div class="prose">
<ul>
  <li>n8n’s own <a href={S.hetznerDoc}>Hetzner guide</a>: “For most usage levels, the CPX11 type is enough” (2 vCPU, 2 GB). If you add <b>n8n Assistant</b>, choose “at least 4 GB RAM / 2 vCPU”.</li>
  <li>n8n’s <a href={S.awsDoc}>Kubernetes guides</a> list the resources behind its own Cloud tiers: Starter 320 MB RAM, Pro (10k executions) 640 MB, Pro (50k executions) 1,280 MB.</li>
  <li>The default Docker setup uses SQLite. n8n’s docs say to use Postgres for production instances with more than a handful of users or round-the-clock workflows. That can run on the same server.</li>
</ul>
<p>My take: a 2 GB server is plenty for a small team’s workflows. I’d go to 4 GB if you want Assistant, plan to run Postgres on the same box, or lean on heavy code steps. That’s my judgment from n8n’s numbers above, not something I load-tested.</p>
</div>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
<h2>Self-hosting: VPS prices that fit</h2>
<div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Host</th><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col">Spec</th><th scope="col">Price</th><th scope="col">Note</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>{vps.map(v => <tr><th scope="row"><a href={v.src} rel="nofollow">{v.host}</a></th><td>{v.plan}</td><td>{v.spec}</td><td>{v.usd ? <><b class="usd">{v.usd}</b><br /><small class="eur">{v.price} (official)</small></> : v.price}</td><td>{v.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="note">Sources, checked {CHECKED}: <a href={S.hzCost}>Hetzner Cost-Optimized</a>, <a href={S.hzReg}>Hetzner Regular Performance</a>, <a href={S.do}>DigitalOcean Droplets</a>, <a href={S.vultr}>Vultr</a>. Hetzner prices are in euros, excluding VAT, and vary by location. {FX_NOTE} Add-ons cost extra: DigitalOcean backups are 20% (weekly) or 30% (daily) of the Droplet price; Vultr automatic backups add 20%. A domain name is extra too.</p>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap prose">
<h2>Cloud vs self-hosted at a glance</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Cheapest Cloud plan:</b> ≈ $22/mo (€20) (Starter, annual).</li>
  <li><b>A 4 GB self-hosted server:</b> $20/mo (Vultr Regular) or $24/mo (DigitalOcean Basic), before backups.</li>
  <li><b>A 2 GB self-hosted server:</b> $10/mo (Vultr Regular) or $12/mo (DigitalOcean Basic).</li>
</ul>
<p>Here’s what surprised me: the server bill on its own is close to the price of Starter. So if Starter’s limits fit you, I don’t think self-hosting is worth it just to save money. It starts to pay off when you’d otherwise need Pro, or when you run a lot of workflows, because Community edition has no execution quota of its own. The server is your only limit.</p>

<h2>License: the Sustainable Use License</h2>
<p>n8n is “fair-code”, not OSI open source. Its <a href={S.license}>Sustainable Use License</a> lets you use and modify n8n “only for your own internal business purposes or for non-commercial or personal use”, and distribute it only “free of charge for non-commercial purposes”. Files marked <code>.ee</code> need a paid Enterprise license. As I read it, running n8n for your own company is fine and reselling it to clients as a hosted service is not. I’m not a lawyer, so if your case is borderline, read the license yourself or ask n8n.</p>

<h2>What you give up by self-hosting</h2>
<p>From n8n’s <a href={S.editions}>edition comparison</a>, the free Community edition does not include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Sharing workflows and credentials (only the owner and creator can access them)</li>
  <li>Projects, custom variables and environments</li>
  <li>SSO (SAML, LDAP), external secrets, log streaming</li>
  <li>Git version control, multi-main mode, external storage for binary data</li>
</ul>
<p>Registering your instance by email (free) adds folders, debug in editor and custom execution data. You also take on the work: n8n’s own guide says self-hosting “requires technical knowledge” and recommends n8n Cloud if you aren’t experienced at managing servers, since “mistakes can lead to data loss, security issues, and downtime”. n8n ships a new minor version most weeks, so budget real time for updates. Honestly, that upkeep is the part people underestimate.</p>
<p>It isn’t all loss, though. Per n8n’s pricing page, running bash scripts and custom nodes only works self-hosted.</p>

<h2>FAQ</h2>
{faq.map(f => <><h3>{f.q}</h3><p>{f.a}</p></>)}
</div></section>

